import re
P0_LBL="What is self-regulation here?|When does a child start controlling herself?|What keeps growing after early childhood?|What changes when a skill runs by itself?|When do children think about their own thinking?|What carries control from outside to inside?|Why don't children use helpful self-talk?|How can a teacher make a strategy last?|What can you now answer?"
P0=[
'''<p class="zero qstart"><span class="qq">How does a baby who controls almost nothing become a child who can steer herself, and why do children sometimes not use a strategy that would help them?</span><b>Why this matters.</b> Operant and information processing theory describe how self-regulation works. Developmental theory asks a different question: how does it <i>grow</i>? The answer tells a teacher what she can expect from a learner of a given age, and why training a strategy once is not enough.<span class="rm"><b>In this step you learn four things:</b><span><i>1</i>When control appears, from birth to about age three</span><span><i>2</i>What keeps growing at school age: attention, memory, thinking about your thinking</span><span><i>3</i>The tool that carries control inside: talking to yourself</span><span><i>4</i>Why children don't use helpful self-talk, and how a teacher fixes it</span></span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> you place milestones on a timeline, predict a memory span, then diagnose three children. Each card tells you which part to play.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Self-regulation is not a switch you turn on. It grows: as children develop, their thinking changes in ways that give them more and more control over their thoughts, feelings and actions.</span>That is how developmental theorists define it (Paris, Byrnes &amp; Paris, 2001). What does "control" mean in practice? Kopp (1982) spells it out: starting and stopping an action, making speech or movement more or less frequent or intense, waiting before acting on a goal, and behaving in ways others approve of. <i>Our example:</i> a toddler sees a biscuit and grabs it; a four-year-old sees the same biscuit and waits until after dinner. Same wish, more control.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> what you can ask of a learner depends on how far her control has grown. Researchers in this tradition study two things: how control changes with age, and the key role of <b>private speech</b>, talking to yourself.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: when does that control first appear?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ The first voluntary control appears at 9 to 12 months, when the baby does what a caregiver asks. From there control grows in phases and moves from outside the child (a parent says "stop") to inside (she stops herself), reaching self-regulation from about 36 months.</span>Kopp (1982) describes the phases. Read them as a ladder where each phase adds a new source of control: first the body, then reactions to the world, then a relationship, then rules, then language, then thought.<span class="rm tl"><span><i>0–3m</i><b>Neurophysiological modulation.</b> The baby controls only her arousal and simple acts such as reaching; maturation and parents' routines, such as feeding, shape her behaviour.</span><span><i>3–9m</i><b>Sensorimotor modulation.</b> Her behaviour changes in response to what happens around her.</span><span><i>9–12m</i><b>Compliance.</b> The first <em>voluntary</em> control: doing what a caregiver asks. It depends on how receptive she is and on the quality of the mother-child relationship.</span><span><i>12–18m</i><b>Impulse control</b> (second year). She notices what a situation demands and can start, continue and stop actions; she starts to act on purpose, towards goals. The second year is the critical shift from outside to inside control (Kochanska et al., 1998): parents discipline more, and her compliance becomes linked to <b>internalizing</b> rules, taking them in as her own.</span><span><i>24–36m</i><b>Self-control</b> (third year). She reacts more to adults' commands, and growing language lets words direct her (the "directive function of speech"); taking in adults' guidance becomes common.</span><span><i>36m+</i><b>Self-regulation</b> (fourth year). She adopts rules that guide her, takes in even more of others' guidance, thinking starts to steer her behaviour, and she adapts when demands change.</span></span><span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> self-regulation is the end of a long path from outside control to inside control. Keep this thread, "outside → inside"; it returns with Vygotsky in the next tab and in reading 3.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> place the four missing phases on the timeline.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: by about three, the basics of control are there. What keeps growing after that?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ By early childhood the basic thinking processes are in place. What grows next is how well and how quickly children use them.</span>Developmental research now looks mostly through an <b>information processing</b> lens: how children take in, store and retrieve information (Samuelson &amp; Smith, 2000). Attention, storing, retrieving and metacognition improve with age, and so does their speed (Kail &amp; Ferrer, 2007). Young children find it hard to keep their attention on one thing, to attend to what matters and ignore the rest, and to switch quickly between activities. As they learn to control attention, their <b>working memory</b> (what you can hold in mind right now) improves. A simple test is the <b>digit span</b>: a researcher reads digits at one per second and the child repeats them. An average 5-year-old repeats four; by 12 it is six or seven (Meece, 2002). Behind this, capacity and strategies work together: as capacity grows, better strategies can be used (Matlin, 2009). Rehearsal (repeating) appears early (Flavell, 1999); organizing and linking information improve with age and can be taught; older children search their memory longer before giving up.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> older children don't just have more room in their heads; they use what they have better.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> predict the memory span.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: what happens when a skill starts to run by itself?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ When a skill becomes automatic, it stops costing attention, so the child's effort is free for the harder part of the task.</span>This is <b>automaticity</b>: a process starts to run by itself, without effort. Less effort is then needed in the early stages of processing: once a child decodes words automatically, her effort is free for understanding the text; poor readers spend so much effort decoding that their comprehension suffers. <i>Our example:</i> a new driver can't chat while changing gears; an experienced one can.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> young learners have limited attention to spend, so teachers warn them what to pay attention to, give outlines and study guides beforehand, and use prompts, questions and feedback to keep them focused (Meece, 2002).</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the change that matters most for self-regulation, thinking about your own thinking.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Metacognition, knowing and steering your own thinking, expands greatly between ages 5 and 10.</span>Metacognition is the "inner coach" of self-regulation, so this growth matters most (Flavell, 1999). Children learn methods for <b>monitoring</b> (checking how well they understand), asking themselves questions about what they read, and summarizing (Dinsmore et al., 2008). They learn which strategy fits which task, and they come to believe that using a strategy improves performance. They do switch to a new strategy, slowly, once it gives more accurate answers again and again (Siegler &amp; Svetina, 2006). <b>Alexander, Carr and Schwanenflugel (1995)</b> found steady improvement with age in metacognitive knowledge and in monitoring and regulating strategy use. <i>In plain words:</i> the inner coach gets better year by year, gradually.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> a teacher can support self-monitoring from outside with <b>self-recording</b>, such as diaries and checklists of the task's steps (Zimmerman, Bonner &amp; Kovach, 1996), for example: read the passage, find the main characters, decide on the main action.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: how does a child's own voice become that inner coach?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Language. Children learn to steer themselves by talking to themselves: private speech.</span><b class="tm" data-g="privsp">Private speech</b> is speech that steers your own behaviour rather than communicating with someone (Fuson, 1979), and cognitive developmental theory links it strongly to the growth of self-regulation (Berk, 1986). The idea goes back to Pavlov (1927), who separated a first, perceptual signal system from a second, language-based one. He thought language and thought explain why humans are conditioned differently from animals: we don't simply react to a stimulus, we interpret it with words and experience. He never studied the second system himself; later research supported him. <b>Luria (1961)</b> traced how words take over a child's actions in three stages. Ages 1½ to 2½: other people's words direct the child. Ages 3 to 4: her own words can start an action but cannot reliably stop it. Ages 4½ to 5½: her private speech can start, direct and stop what she does. Luria believed this inner speech steers behaviour through neurophysiological (brain) mechanisms. <i>Our example:</i> a child doing a puzzle says "corners first… no, not that one", and her hand follows her words.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> the voice that guides the child moves from her parents to her own head. It is the same outside-to-inside thread as Kopp's phases, now with words as the tool. Vygotsky's theory builds this second signal system in; you meet it in the next tab.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: if self-talk helps, why don't children always use it?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Three different problems: the child doesn't say it, says it but it doesn't steer her, or stops saying it once nobody asks her to.</span><b class="tm" data-g="prodef">Production deficiency:</b> she doesn't produce task-relevant words (a rule, a strategy, what to remember) when they would help. <b class="tm" data-g="meddef">Mediational deficiency:</b> she says them, but they don't change what she does next (Fuson, 1979). Young children often talk without it steering them; later their words can steer them, but they don't always talk at the right moment. This neat order fits simple self-talk, such as repeating a list; with complex self-talk both problems can occur together. <b class="tm" data-g="contdef">Continued-use deficiency:</b> trained children often stop talking themselves through a task once they are no longer asked to (Schunk, 1982). <i>Our examples:</i> one child stays silent during a memory task (production); another keeps saying "carry the one" but never carries (mediational); a third used her checklist in training and dropped it the week after (continued use).<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> "she doesn't use the strategy" has three different causes, and each needs a different fix.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> diagnose the three children.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: how a teacher makes a trained strategy last.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Find out why the child stopped, then fix that cause: too little practice, a strategy stuck to one task, or no belief that it helps.</span>The chapter names three causes of a continued-use deficiency, each with its remedy. <b>1. She doesn't understand the strategy well enough</b>, after too little instruction and practice → repeated instruction and practice, with spaced review sessions. <b>2. She ties it to the training task</b> and doesn't see how to use it elsewhere → train on several tasks, and teach explicitly how to adapt the strategy. <b>3. She doesn't believe it improves her performance</b>; she may think effort or available time matter more (Fabricius &amp; Hagen, 1984) → give <b>strategy value information</b> (Baker &amp; Brown, 1984; Schunk &amp; Rice, 1987): tell her the strategy will help, that it helped other students, and give feedback that links her strategy use to her progress. Research shows that strategy value information improves performance, continued use and transfer to other tasks (Lodico et al., 1983; Paris, Newman &amp; McVey, 1982).<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> teaching a strategy is not enough; students also need to know why and when it works. Cause and remedy pairs are a classic exam question for this step.</span><span class="bridge">→ Last card: what you can now answer.</span></p>''',
'''<p class="recap"><span class="ans">⭐ Developmental theory in one line: self-regulation grows from outside control to inside control, and language carries it inside.</span><span class="rm"><span><i>✓</i><b>What is self-regulation here?</b> Progressive changes in thinking that give more control over thoughts, feelings and actions.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>When does control appear?</b> Kopp's phases, from arousal (0 to 3 months) to self-regulation (from 36 months); the second year is the shift from outside to inside.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>What keeps growing?</b> Attention, working memory (digit span four at 5, six or seven at 12), automaticity, and metacognition (big growth from 5 to 10).</span><span><i>✓</i><b>What carries control inside?</b> Private speech, in Luria's three stages.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>Why isn't helpful self-talk used?</b> Production, mediational and continued-use deficiencies, and the three causes and remedies.</span></span><b>Typical exam shapes:</b> place a child's behaviour in a phase; diagnose which deficiency a case shows; name the remedy for a given cause.<span class="bridge">→ Next tab: ② Social constructivist. Vygotsky takes the same idea, words moving inside, further.</span></p>''']
P1_LBL="What do social constructivists assume?|What theories do learners build, and can they be wrong?|What do learners believe about their own ability?|How does identity shape self-regulation?|How does talking to yourself change with age?|How does help from others become your own skill?|What can you now answer?"
P1=[
'''<p class="zero qstart"><span class="qq">If learners build their own knowledge, what do they build about themselves, and how does help from others become their own skill?</span><b>Why this matters.</b> Developmental theory showed what grows with age. Social constructivism adds that learners are not passive: they actively build theories about their abilities, their tasks and their strategies, and those theories, right or wrong, steer how they regulate themselves.<span class="rm"><b>In this tab you learn four things:</b><span><i>1</i>The five assumptions of social constructivism</span><span><i>2</i>The theories learners build, and how they go wrong</span><span><i>3</i>Mindsets and the identity as a student</span><span><i>4</i>Vygotsky: private speech and the zone of proximal development</span></span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> you judge four things learners built, place three tasks in the zones of Lea, our example learner, then pick Vygotsky's curve.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Learners build their own knowledge, driven from within, and how far they can build depends on their development.</span>The chapter names five assumptions (Paris &amp; Byrnes, 1989; Paris et al., 2001). <b>1. People are intrinsically motivated to learn:</b> from birth they explore and try to understand, and they give meaning to what they see based on their experience. <b>2. Mental representations change with development:</b> the way you hold knowledge in mind changes; infants think in actions and images, older learners use their culture's tools, such as language and symbols. <b>3. Understanding keeps being refined:</b> fitting what you know with what you meet never ends, pushed by reflection, experience, others' guidance and new information. <b>4. Development limits learning:</b> <b>readiness</b> is maturation plus prior experience, and learning works best when new information fits it. <b>5. Reflection and reconstruction drive learning:</b> teaching can help, but the main motor is the learner's own need to rethink.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> together they say the drive is inside (1, 5), what is built changes with age (2, 4), and building never stops (3). So self-regulation must be something the learner builds, not something she is given: theories about what she can do and why.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: which theories, and what if they are wrong?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Learners build theories about their abilities, about tasks, and about how to use effort and strategies, and these theories can be wrong.</span>The theories change with development. Take <b>attributions</b>, the causes you give for your results. Young children's attributions do not separate ability from effort; a distinct idea of ability appears later (Nicholls, 1978), and then children realize that performance may not match ability and that effort and help also matter. Children also build their own versions of the strategies they are taught. Knowing a strategy includes its goal, the tasks it suits, how it helps and how much effort it costs (Borkowski et al., 1987). Most strategies suit specific tasks, but some, such as setting goals and checking progress, work across many (Pressley et al., 1990; Zimmerman, 2011). Because theories are built on incomplete information, they can go wrong. A <b class="tm" data-g="buggy">buggy algorithm</b> is such a wrong strategy (Brown &amp; Burton, 1978): always subtracting the smaller digit from the larger in each column. <i>Our example:</i> 52 − 17 becomes 45 instead of 35. The method still produces answers, so the child feels capable: a <b>false sense of self-efficacy</b> (self-efficacy is your belief about what you are capable of): a big gap between what she believes she can do and what she can do.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> a wrong theory steers behaviour as surely as a right one, so a teacher needs to find out what students believe, not only what they do.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> judge the four constructions.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: the theories learners build about themselves.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Learners build a mindset: a theory about whether their ability to learn can grow. It decides whether effort and new strategies seem worth it.</span>Dweck's <b class="tm" data-g="mindset">mindsets</b> are theories about your own capacity to learn. With a <b>fixed mindset</b> you believe what you can learn is limited, and no effort or help can change that; with a <b>growth mindset</b> you believe you can keep improving. Growth-mindset learners engage more productively and persevere when they meet obstacles (Dweck, 2006). Why this matters for regulation, in our words: a learner who thinks effort is useless has no reason to plan, try a new strategy or keep going when it gets hard. These self-theories come partly from teachers, peers and parents, and largely from reflecting on your own results, your surroundings and others' reactions. They are built with the culture's tools (language, signs, symbols), in social settings, inside the zone of proximal development you meet later in this tab.<span class="bridge">→ Next: from beliefs about your ability to beliefs about who you are.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Learners build an identity as a student, and how they study expresses who they believe they are or want to become.</span>The end goal of all this theory building is an <b>identity as a student</b>, which others influence, including stereotypes about gender, culture and ethnic background (Graham &amp; Williams, 2009). Paris et al. (2001) argue that identity and self-regulated learning cannot be separated: how you study shows who you believe you are or want to become, so strategies cannot be taught apart from a student's goals, roles and identity.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> successful learners build theories that help them; unsuccessful ones may build unhelpful goals and strategies. Self-regulation depends heavily on how children see themselves and their tasks (Dweck &amp; Master, 2008).</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: Vygotsky, and how words from others become your own.</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Vygotsky expected thinking aloud to rise until about 6 or 7 and then go inward; research found a messier pattern.</span>Vygotsky, a Russian psychologist, gave language the central role in self-regulation: private speech helps develop thought by <b>organizing behaviour</b> (Vygotsky, 1962). It is Pavlov's and Luria's second signal system again. Children use private speech to understand situations and get past difficulties, together with their social interactions; they take in the meaning of others' words and use them to steer themselves. He predicted a <b>curvilinear</b> pattern (up, then down): thinking aloud increases until ages 6 to 7, then declines and is mostly silent and inner by 8 to 10, although anyone may think aloud again when stuck. Research (Fuson, 1979): from about 4 or 5 to 8, the <b>amount</b> of private speech goes down, but the <b>share</b> of it that is self-regulating goes up. In many studies the amount is small and many children don't talk aloud at all. <i>Our example:</i> an adult muttering "this screw, then that one" while assembling a cupboard.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> the direction (words moving inside) holds; the neat curve does not.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> pick Vygotsky's curve.</span><span class="bridge">→ Next: how does help from others become your own skill?</span></p>''',
'''<p><span class="ans">⭐ Through the zone of proximal development: help is given just beyond what the learner can do alone; as she takes the skill in, she works more on her own and the zone moves up.</span>The <b class="tm" data-g="zpd">zone of proximal development</b> is the amount of learning possible with the right instruction: the tasks a learner cannot do alone but can do with some help. Teachers or peers give <b>scaffolding</b>, temporary support; as the learner internalizes the skill, she works more and more on her own, and the zone moves on to harder learning. <i>Our example:</i> learning to ride a bike with a hand on the saddle, until the hand lets go without you noticing. <i>Our extension:</i> tasks she can already do alone lie below the zone; tasks she cannot do even with help lie beyond it.<span class="soth"><b>So this means:</b> self-regulation is internalizing skills and beliefs so that you can handle new learning situations on your own. The outside → inside thread again, now with a helper.</span><span class="look">👀 <b>In the picture:</b> place the three tasks in Lea's zones.</span><span class="bridge">→ Last card: what you can now answer.</span></p>''',
'''<p class="recap"><span class="ans">⭐ Social constructivism in one line: learners build theories about themselves, tasks and strategies, and help from others becomes their own through language and the zone of proximal development.</span><span class="rm"><span><i>✓</i><b>What do they assume?</b> Intrinsic motivation, changing representations, endless refinement, readiness limits learning, reflection drives it.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>What theories do learners build?</b> About ability, tasks and strategies (a strategy's goal, tasks, how it helps, effort cost); wrong ones like a buggy algorithm give a false sense of self-efficacy.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>What about the self?</b> Fixed or growth mindset, and an identity as a student that cannot be separated from self-regulation.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>Vygotsky's curve?</b> Predicted up until 6 or 7, then inward; research: less private speech, but a larger self-regulating share.</span><span><i>✓</i><b>The zone?</b> What you can do with help but not yet alone; with scaffolding you work more and more alone as you internalize, and the zone moves up.</span></span><b>Typical exam shapes:</b> explain how a buggy algorithm leads to false self-efficacy; judge whether research confirmed Vygotsky's curve; decide whether a task is in a learner's zone.<span class="bridge">→ Next step: ⑥ Social cognitive theory, the theory that runs through this whole theme.</span></p>''']
def rebuild(pane_html,lbl,cards):
    i=pane_html.index('<div class="teach"');j=pane_html.index('<div class="trapb">',i)
    head=pane_html[i:j]
    k=head.index('</div>',head.index('class="out"'))+6  # end of out div
    new=re.sub(r'data-lbl="[^"]*"','data-lbl="'+lbl+'"',head[:k],1)+''.join(cards)
    return pane_html[:i]+new+pane_html[j:]
def apply(path):
    s=open(path,encoding='utf-8').read()
    parts=s.split('<section class="step"');st=parts[5]
    a=st.index('<div class="pane on" data-pane="0">');b=st.index('<div class="pane" data-pane="1">')
    st=st[:a]+rebuild(st[a:b],P0_LBL,P0)+rebuild(st[b:],P1_LBL,P1)
    parts[5]=st;open(path,'w',encoding='utf-8').write('<section class="step"'.join(parts))
    for lbl,cards in ((P0_LBL,P0),(P1_LBL,P1)):
        assert len(lbl.split('|'))==len(cards)-1,(len(lbl.split('|')),len(cards))
    return 'ok'
